so things  like installation or configuration or  affect an actual bug that someone found  and may graph those over time as well so  that we can tell we're category to need  the most love in the products and things  like that so this is a really practical  application that we use in-house every  day to figure out where we can target  improvements based on information that  we're pulling in from a couple of  different API is actually that we pull  together using the tools that I have  just described  so to recap I can't emphasize enough how  useful a PRS are especially for datasets  that are updated regularly again I don't  touch that script we deployed it to  connect and it runs every night without  me touching it and pulls all that data  in by itself based on the code that I've  written to handle that API very easy to  pull that data in keep it up to date  with the right packages and tools you  are going to have to do some works  around you know the API contract to make  sure you understand what's coming back  and to handle errors and things like  that but once you get it in place it's  very easy to maintain and once you have  that data in or you can do all sorts of  wonderful things like with it in our  case we're making business decisions off  of it so it's pretty exciting stuff if  you want more information there's more  information on APA pis and HTTP itself  in these two links these two links are  also available in Hadleys getting  started vignette four-hitter so you  don't feel you have to snag it here  although I think these slides will be  available online for the webinars later  and if you need more information on the  tools themselves you can always run the  help function there's a really great  documentation there haffley's QuickStart  vignette vignette I've mentioned before  and his demos in the master in his in  the repo and the github repo this JSON  light vignettes are also really really  helpful I assume there's also XML two  vignettes that I neglected to look up  but I assume you can find them as well  if your data is coming back as XML and  then the the last one here is a really  interesting vignette on how to use this  information to write your own package to  access an API if you have an API out  there where you have an internal API for  that matter and you want to write a  package to make it easier for people to  access this information that has  error-handling built-in things like that  this vignette gives some best practices  for how to do that finally if you have  any questions that aren't answered here  you're always welcome to to write to me  directly I'm Amanda at our studio comm  well thank you so much Amanda that was  terrific our audience has any questions  please use the question box in the  system to pose those now but as we wait  for that are there any other data  sources that you would recommend for our  experimentation something sure sure  well I'm very partial to the Star Wars  API as you can tell there are a couple  of other ones as well there's one called  om GD which we touched on briefly at the  beginning that's the online movie  database it's like an open source  version of IMDB and it's very structured  like this is so you can play around with  that very easily without worrying about  too many too many frustrations in the  API there's also one called  HTTP bin org HTTP bi n org and that is a  very simple simple way to test your  these different methods out with very  simple responses it is very very  bare-bones but it's a great way to get  sort of some extent practice and their  documentation is excellent as well  [Music]

